{"reason": "Execution results show clear overfitting (train loss 0.36 vs MA-RAE ~2.5) and plateauing MA-RAE around 2.48, validating the initial plan's diagnosis. The successful historical experiment (improved MA-RAE from 2.8 to 2.1) used GIN with SMILES augmentation and separate heads, confirming that augmentation and task-specific attention are effective. The failed experiment (modified MLP with dropout and weighted loss) only achieved MA-RAE 2.6, indicating that model architecture changes alone are insufficient. Therefore, I focus on data augmentation (to increase diversity) and model architecture (task-specific attention) to leverage the synergy identified in the initial plan. The training module remains unchanged because the scheduler and optimizer are already effective based on the historical success.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add on-the-fly SMILES augmentation during training in the DataLoader or Dataset wrapper. For each molecule, with 50% probability, generate a randomized SMILES using RDKit's Chem.MolToSmiles(mol, doRandom=True, canonical=False), then re-extract features from the augmented SMILES before feeding to the model. This increases effective training diversity, preventing memorization and improving generalization. The augmentation is applied only during training (not validation/test) and uses the existing feature extraction pipeline.", "model_design": "Add task-specific attention after the shared encoder (output dim 256). For each of the 9 tasks, introduce a learnable query vector (dim=256) that computes attention weights over the shared representation via scaled dot-product: attn_weights_i = softmax(query_i @ shared / sqrt(256)). The weighted representation is then fed into each task's head (128→1). This allows each task to selectively amplify relevant feature dimensions, improving performance on sparse tasks. Keep all other model components unchanged (shared encoder, heads, loss function)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
